{
  "dom_id": "step-q334111",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
